
{"id":"e191030b-6b3d-4e5b-9432-70fe76c808a6","object":"chat.completion","created":1784986206,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    id_to_node = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = n['id']\\n            break\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    removed = set()\\n    removal_order = []\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid in removed:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\\n            if cid not in removed:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        symbol = node['symbol']\\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\\n            other_exists = False\\n            for other in nodes:\\n                if other['id'] != nid and other['symbol'] == symbol and other['id'] not in removed:\\n                    other_exists = True\\n                    break\\n            if not other_exists:\\n                continue\\n        removed.add(nid)\\n        removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed])\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removal_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The task appears to be grammar-guided node reduction. Based on the fixture payload, we have nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. And required_symbols. We need to remove some nodes (those with removable=True) while preserving required symbols and possibly other constraints. The description mentions \"grammar-guided deletion and replacement candidates\", \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\", \"deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\", \"never remove root, required symbol, or node with retained children\", \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\", \"use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth\", \"return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order\". Also \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" are required output keys.\n\nSo algorithm: We have a tree defined by parent references. Nodes have ids, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable flag, utility. The required_symbols list specifies symbols that must be present in the remaining tree after removal. We need to decide which removable nodes to remove, in some order, while ensuring that after removal, all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., at least one node with that symbol remains). Also we need to consider that we cannot remove a node that has retained children? Actually \"never remove a node with retained children\" means if a node is removed, its children must also be removed (since parent removed). But the phrasing \"retained children\" might mean children that are not removed? Probably we cannot remove a node if it still has children that we intend to keep. In typical tree pruning, removing a node removes its entire subtree. So we need to ensure that if we remove a node, we also remove all its descendants. The requirement \"never remove a node with retained children\" likely means that we should not remove a node if any of its children are going to be retained (i.e., not removed). So we must either remove the entire subtree or keep the node. So removal decisions are cascading: if we remove a node, all its descendants are removed too. But we only list removal of nodes individually? The output removed_node_ids should list all removed nodes (including descendants). The \"actual removal order\" likely means the order in which nodes are removed, processing deeper nodes first. So we need to traverse nodes in decreasing depth, then tie-break by ID (maybe ascending? but \"node ID as stable tie break at equal depth\" - typical tie-break by alphabetical or something). We'll decide to sort by depth descending, then by ID ascending (or descending? We'll assume ascending as stable). Then for each removable node, check if it can be removed while preserving required symbols. But we also need to consider that removing a node also removes its descendants. So when we tentatively remove a node, we need to check if all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree (i.e., after removing this node and all its descendants). Also we should ensure that no prerequisite symbols are lost. Also root is not removable; required_symbols includes \"root\" always? In examples, root is required. Also other required symbols like \"s1\". So we must ensure that at least one node with symbol from required_symbols remains after removal.\n\nEdge cases: Node with removable=False cannot be removed. So we only consider removable=True nodes. Also nodes with removable=True but are required? Not directly, but if the node's symbol is required, we cannot remove it because that would remove that symbol. So we need to check that removal does not eliminate the last node of a required symbol. Also we need to consider that we may have multiple nodes with same symbol; we can remove some as long as at least one remains.\n\nWe also have utility? Not used in constraints, perhaps used for ordering? The description mentions \"candidate_order\" but not clearly. We'll ignore utility for now.\n\nSo algorithm steps:\n1. Build adjacency from parent references: for each node, children list.\n2. Build a map id->node.\n3. Compute depth from root (provided).\n4. Build set of required_symbols.\n5. Create a set of removed IDs initially empty.\n6. Create a list of removal order.\n7. Get all removable nodes (removable=True) that are not root and not having required symbol? Actually we can consider all removable nodes, but we need to check if removal would remove all instances of a required symbol.\n8. Sort removable nodes by depth descending, then by id string ascending (stable tie-break).\n9. For each node in that order:\n   - If node.id already in removed, skip.\n   - Tentatively assume we remove this node and all its descendants (i.e., entire subtree). We need to compute the set of nodes that would be removed (including self and all descendants recursively). But we have to consider that some descendants might have been already removed earlier? Since we process deeper first, some descendants might have been removed already. Actually if we remove a deeper node first, its parent might still be present. Then later when we consider the parent, removing it would also remove the child that is already removed - that's fine. But we need to handle that if a child is already removed, removing the parent doesn't double-count. However, the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" suggests we should not remove a parent if any child is retained. So we need to check if any descendant that is not already removed (i.e., would be retained) would be removed by this decision. That is, we must ensure that after removal, no retained children exist. But since we are removing the entire subtree, any descendants that are still present will be removed; that's fine. The constraint is that we cannot remove a parent while keeping a child. So we need to ensure that all children are either already removed or will be removed as part of this action. Since we remove the whole subtree, any child that is still present will be removed, so it's okay. However, if a child has removable=False, we cannot remove it. So we must ensure that we do not attempt to remove a node that has a non-removable descendant. Because removing the parent would implicitly remove that non-removable child, which violates \"never remove a required symbol\"? Actually non-removable nodes cannot be removed. But the removal of a parent would cause that child to be removed implicitly. So we must not remove a parent if any descendant is non-removable (unless that descendant is already removed? But non-removable cannot be removed, so it must stay). So effectively, we should only consider removing nodes whose entire subtree consists of removable nodes (or already removed). But note: The problem statement says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That \"node with retained children\" means if after removal, the node's children would be retained? Actually if we remove a node, its children are not retained (they are removed). So the condition likely means: do not remove a node if any of its children are going to be kept (i.e., not removed). So if we are removing a node, we must also remove all its children. So that's fine. But children that are non-removable cannot be removed, so we cannot remove the parent. So we must check that all descendants are removable (or already removed). But also we need to check that removing the subtree does not eliminate all nodes of a required symbol.\n\nGiven the complexity, we should implement a function that given a set of nodes to be removed (the subtree), checks if the resulting tree still contains at least one node for each required symbol. Also check that no non-removable node is in the subtree to be removed. Since we process nodes in depth order, we can build a set of \"still present\" nodes initially all nodes. Then for each candidate removal, compute the subtree to remove (including all nodes that are still present in that subtree). Then check: if any node in that subtree has removable=False, skip. Also check required_symbols: after removing that subtree, is there still at least one node for each symbol? If yes, then we remove it (add to removed_ids set, and add to removal order). Also we need to update the \"still present\" set by removing those nodes.\n\nWe need to ensure we only consider nodes that are currently present (not yet removed). Also we need to ensure that we don't remove root.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build tree: for each node, find children.\n- Compute descendants: we can compute for each node its set of all descendants (or compute on the fly using DFS).\n- We'll use a set `present` of node IDs initially all nodes.\n- Iterate candidate nodes sorted by depth desc, id asc. For each node in candidates (where node.id in present and node.removable and node.id != root):\n   - Compute the set of nodes in its subtree that are still present (including node). We can do a BFS/DFS ignoring nodes already removed.\n   - Check: if any of those nodes has removable==False (and is not root? root is not removable anyway), skip.\n   - Check required symbols: compute set of symbols from present nodes not in subtree. Are all required symbols still represented? (i.e., for each req symbol, at least one node with that symbol remains in present - subtree).\n   - If both conditions hold, then remove: add subtree IDs to removed, append node.id to removal order (or all removed IDs? The output requires removed_node_ids list; it should contain all removed IDs, not just the ones we decided to remove. The \"actual removal order\" might refer to the order in which we decided to remove nodes (the parents). But the output only asks for removed_node_ids (a list) and retained_node_ids. No explicit order for removed. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" But the output schema only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids (both arrays). It doesn't specify order. \"sorted retained IDs\" suggests retained_node_ids should be sorted (maybe alphabetically). \"actual removal order\" suggests removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed. But schema doesn't specify order. However the instruction says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we should sort retained_node_ids, and removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the order we processed them). In the algorithm, we remove nodes one by one (with cascade), so we can append the node id to a removal_order list each time we decide to remove a node. Then after processing all, the removed_node_ids list will be the removal order (including all nodes that got removed). But note: when we remove a parent, its descendants also get removed, but we didn't explicitly decide to remove them individually. Should we include them in the removal order? In the output, removed_node_ids should contain all removed node IDs. The order could be any order, but \"actual removal order\" implies the order in which decisions were made, i.e., the order of the parent removals, and then descendants implicitly. But the output expects a list of all removed IDs. To preserve \"actual removal order\", we could produce the list in the order that nodes were marked as removed. That is, when we remove a subtree, we add all the nodes in that subtree to the removed set, but we also need to record the order. We can maintain a list `removed_order` that we extend with the subtree nodes in the order of processing (starting with the candidate node, then its descendants in some order). However, the fixture examples don't show expected outputs. We need to infer from the task description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" This suggests removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal decisions. The simplest approach: when we decide to remove a node, we add that node's ID to removal_order list (not its descendants). Then after processing all, the removed_node_ids will be the list of all nodes that were removed (including descendants). But we need to include descendants in the output. The output schema requires an array of all removed node IDs. So we must list all nodes that are ultimately removed, not just the decision points. The \"actual removal order\" could still be represented by the order in which they were removed, which may be depth-first: we remove deeper nodes first, then parents. In our algorithm, we remove a parent later, but its descendants were already removed earlier (if we process deeper first). That would cause descendants to appear before parent in removal order, which is inconsistent with the idea of cascading removal. Alternatively, we might decide to remove a parent only after checking that its descendants are all removable, but we do not remove them individually; we remove the parent and all its descendants at once (in a single step). In that case, the removal order should list the parent first? The description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That implies we consider deeper nodes first for removal. So we might remove a leaf first, then later its parent. That would mean we remove individual nodes one by one. Let's re-evaluate: The task says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That suggests we consider each node individually for removal. And \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. That means we must remove children first before removing parent. So the algorithm is: iterate nodes in decreasing depth, for each removable node, if its children (if any) are all already removed (or not present) and required symbols are still present after its removal, then remove it. This is typical bottom-up reduction. So we remove nodes one by one, not entire subtrees at once. When we remove a node, its children are already removed (or were never present because they were removable and we removed them earlier). So the condition \"node with retained children\" is satisfied because children are already removed. Also, we should not remove a node if it has non-removable children. But if a child is non-removable, it cannot be removed, so the parent cannot be removed. So we need to check that all children are either removable and already removed, or the node is a leaf.\n\nThus, more precise algorithm:\n- Initialize `removed = set()` and `removal_order = []`.\n- For each node in nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID ascending:\n   - if node.id in removed: continue\n   - if not node.removable: continue\n   - if node.id == root: continue (never remove root) – but root has removable=false anyway.\n   - Check if all children of node are either removed or are not present (i.e., leaf). Actually children that are still present (not removed) would be retained. So we require that node has no children that are currently present (i.e., not removed). Because if any child is present, it would be retained after removing node, which violates \"node with retained children\". So we only allow removal if node has no children that are not removed.\n   - If condition holds, then tentatively remove node: check required symbols. The current set of present nodes is all nodes minus removed. After removing node, the set becomes present - {node.id}. Does it still contain at least one node for each required symbol? If yes, then:\n        - add node.id to removed set\n        - append node.id to removal_order\n   - Else, skip.\n\nAfter processing all, retained_node_ids = sorted([n.id for n in nodes if n.id not in removed])\nremoved_node_ids = removal_order (since it's the order of removal decisions). But we also need to include any nodes that were removed indirectly? No, we only removed nodes individually. So all removed nodes are exactly the ones we decided to remove. That should match.\n\nBut consider nodes that are removed because their parent was removed? Not in this algorithm because we remove children first, then parent; parent removal does not cause removal of children because children are already removed. So all removals are explicit decisions. That's fine.\n\nBut what about nodes that are descendants but not removable? They cannot be removed, so they must be retained. If a non-removable child exists, its parent cannot be removed. So that works.\n\nNow check required symbols: we must ensure that after removing node, there is still at least one node for each required symbol. Since we only remove one node at a time, we can check if the node being removed has a required symbol: if yes, we need to ensure there is another node with that symbol still present. If not, we skip.\n\nEdge: If a node's removal would cause a required symbol to disappear, we cannot remove it.\n\nAlso note: we are processing deeper first, so when we consider a node, its children are already processed and possibly removed. So condition of \"no children present\" is easy to check.\n\nNow we need to implement this without mutating input; we just use the data.\n\nLet's test with the first fixture case:\nNodes:\nroot (depth0, not removable, symbol root)\nn1 (depth1, not removable, symbol s1)\nn2 (depth1, removable, symbol s2)\nn3 (depth1, not removable, symbol s0)\nn4 (depth2, removable, symbol s1, parent n2)\nn5 (depth2, not removable, symbol s2, parent n3)\nn6 (depth2, removable, symbol s0, parent n1)\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\",\"s1\"]\n\nRemovable nodes: n2, n4, n6. (n5 not removable)\n\nSorted by depth desc then ID: depth2: n4, n6 (assuming ID ascending: n4 then n6); depth1: n2.\n\nProcess:\n- n4: removable, parent n2. Children? none (leaf). Check required: n4 symbol s1. Is there another node with s1? n1 also has s1. So after removing n4, s1 still present (n1). Also root present. So remove n4. removed = {n4}, order=[n4].\n- n6: removable, parent n1. Leaf. symbol s0, not required. So remove. removed={n4,n6}, order=[n4,n6].\n- n2: removable, parent root. Children? n4 is removed, so no children present. So condition satisfied. symbol s2 not required. Remove n2. removed={n4,n6,n2}, order=[n4,n6,n2].\n\nNow retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5 (and maybe n2? removed). Check required symbols: root, s1 (n1). Yes.\n\nSo output: removed_node_ids = [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"] (in removal order), retained_node_ids sorted: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"] (alphabetical? \"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\" - but root might be sorted as \"root\" after n5? ASCII: 'n' vs 'r' - 'n' < 'r', so order: n1, n3, n5, root. That seems fine.\n\nBut is this the intended result? Not sure. The fixture likely has expected outputs, but we don't have them. We'll trust this algorithm.\n\nNow we need to handle the case where a node has required symbol and no other node with that symbol remains. Then skip removal.\n\nAlso need to consider that root is always required and has symbol \"root\". The required_symbols list includes \"root\". So root cannot be removed. But root has removable=false anyway.\n\nNow implement solve(case) that returns dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe need to be careful: the case payload may have additional fields like \"_case_nonce\". We'll ignore.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    id_to_node = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build children list\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    # Determine root: node with parent None\n    root_id = None\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = n['id']\n            break\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed = set()\n    removal_order = []\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check that no children are still present (not removed)\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for cid in children.get(nid, []):\n            if cid not in removed:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols after removal\n        # Compute set of symbols from nodes that are not removed and not this node\n        # We can do a quick check: for each required symbol, is there at least one node with that symbol other than this node?\n        # Or compute symbols in present set before removal.\n        # Since we only remove one node at a time, we can check if node's symbol is required and if there is another node with that symbol still present.\n        # But need also check if removal of this node could affect other required symbols? No, only its own symbol.\n        # However, we need to ensure that after removal, the root symbol is still present (root is never removed, so fine).\n        # If node's symbol is not required, then removal is fine regarding required symbols.\n        symbol = node['symbol']\n        if symbol in required_symbols:\n            # check if there is another node with this symbol not removed\n            other_exists = False\n            for other in nodes:\n                if other['id'] != nid and other['symbol'] == symbol and other['id'] not in removed:\n                    other_exists = True\n                    break\n            if not other_exists:\n                continue\n        # All conditions satisfied\n        removed.add(nid)\n        removal_order.append(nid)\n    # After loop, compute retained ids\n    retained = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] not in removed])\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removal_order,  # order of removal\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained\n    }\n\nBut wait: The condition \"no children present\" must also consider that children may be non-removable but still present. If a child is non-removable and present, we cannot remove the parent. Our check already catches that because child not in removed. So parent removal skipped. That's correct.\n\nNow we also need to ensure that we don't remove a node that is the only node of a required symbol even if the symbol is not its own? That's covered.\n\nNow we need to test on the three fixture cases to see if any edge cases. The algorithm seems plausible.\n\nOne nuance: The algorithm removes nodes in bottom-up order. But what about nodes that are siblings? The order among siblings by ID affects which gets removed first. That's fine.\n\nNow some potential issues: The input might have nodes with depth not matching? We trust depth.\n\nNow implement as a single function.\n\nWe also need to include the solve function in the implementation string.\n\nWe must output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" whose value is a string containing the Python source.\n\nWe need to escape the string properly.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nWe need to be careful about quoting.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2201,"completion_tokens":5448,"total_tokens":7649,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2176},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5020},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2176,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":25},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}